# SYNOPSIS

```swift
enum Counter {
  static var packetType: UInt32 { get }
  static var changePacketType: UInt32 { get }

  static func create() throws(Status) -> Handle

  @discardableResult
  static func add(_ items: inout [CounterAdd]) throws(Status) -> Int
  static func add(_ counter: borrowing Handle, _ amount: Int64)
    throws(Status)

  static func read(_ counter: borrowing Handle) throws(Status) -> Int64

  static func wait(_ counter: borrowing Handle, until value: Int64,
                   deadline: Int64 = infiniteDeadline) throws(Status)
}
```

# DESCRIPTION

A counter is a signed 64-bit value that its holders add to.
It asserts `Signals.positive` or `Signals.nonPositive` according to its sign.
It is waited on at a value, as a fence, or at every add, as a doorbell.

`create` returns a counter.
It is `counter_create(2)`.

`add` adds an amount to a counter.
With an array of `CounterAdd`, each item holds a counter handle and an amount; the call goes on past an item that fails, writes each item's status back, and returns how many succeeded.
A service rings the doorbells of all its clients in one call this way.
With one counter and an amount, it throws the item's status if the add failed; `outOfRange` means the sum would overflow, and the value is unchanged.
It is `counter_add(2)`.

`read` returns the current value (`counter_read(2)`).

`wait(until:)` returns once the value is at least `value`, or throws `timedOut` at the monotonic `deadline`.
It is a binding on the calling thread's own port.

To receive packets instead of blocking, bind the counter to a port with `Binding` (see sys-port(2)).
A packet of type `packetType` reports a counter reaching a value: `payload.0` is the value waited for, `payload.1` the value reached and `payload.2` the time.
A packet of type `changePacketType` reports adds: `payload.1` is the value, `payload.2` the number of adds coalesced and `payload.3` the time.
A counter can lend a scheduling profile to the threads it wakes; see sys-profile(2).

